1)Diff between container and vm
Containers are lightweight, share the host OS kernel, start in seconds, and are ideal for microservices and cloud-native applications.
VMs run a full OS on a hypervisor, making them more secure but heavier and slower. 
Containers focus on speed and portability, while VMs focus on strong isolation and complete OS environments.”

2)What are Init containers and when do you use them?
“Init containers are specialized containers that run before the main container in a Pod. 
They are used for tasks like setting up configuration, checking dependencies, running migrations, or preparing data.
Each init container must complete successfully before the next one runs, ensuring the application starts only when the environment is fully ready.”
